{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Xkxhf73zXyHbwoAGMp4SsP","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"site:aclrollingreview.org reviewform soundness excitement overall score scale\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Review Form\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewform\"},{\"title\":\"ARR Reviewer Guidelines\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewerguidelines\"},{\"title\":\"ARR Area Chair Guidelines\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/acguidelines\"},{\"title\":\"Response to ACL 2023 Questions\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/ACL-2023-session/\"},{\"title\":\"Authors Guidelines\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/authors\"},{\"title\":\"Incentives 2026\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/incentives2026\"},{\"title\":\"ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/\"},{\"title\":\"ARR Changelog\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/changelog\"},{\"title\":\"ARR SAC Guidelines\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/sacguidelines\"},{\"title\":\"Stats\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/stats/\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found information about the ACL Rolling Review (ARR) review form scales for soundness, excitement, and overall score:\n\n## Soundness Scale\n\nThe soundness scores include: 5 = Excellent (one of the most thorough studies given its type), 4 = Strong (provides sufficient support for all claims), and 3 = Acceptable (provides sufficient support for main claims).\n\n## Excitement Scale\n\nThe excitement scale ranges from 5 = Highly Exciting (would recommend the paper to others and/or attend its presentation) to 4 = Exciting (would mention the paper to others and/or make an effort to attend), down to 1 = Not Exciting (does not resonate and wouldn't resonate with the *ACL community).\n\nExcitement is subjective and does not necessarily follow what is popular in the field; reviewers may perceive papers as transformational/innovative/surprising because they present conceptual breakthroughs or challenge common assumptions, or because of their potential impact on communities through lowering barriers and enabling new applications.\n\n## Overall Score\n\nStarting with February 2025, ARR reviewers are asked to provide three scores: soundness, excitement, and overall recommendation. The Overall assessment score is an explicit recommendation for the outcome of this paper, if it were committed to an *ACL venue, and is a composite score reflecting your assessment of soundness, excitement, and also other factors like novelty and impact.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2dbb1532-cce8-453c-a0ba-ff5d2a8428c3","uuid":"95825c45-b710-494f-a3d7-e1d22b183eee","timestamp":"2026-05-16T10:17:03.697Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"site:aclrollingreview.org reviewform soundness excitement overall score scale","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_012eipNThj4iNPnLirWcu3Ry","content":[{"title":"Review Form","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewform"},{"title":"ARR Reviewer Guidelines","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewerguidelines"},{"title":"ARR Area Chair Guidelines","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/acguidelines"},{"title":"Response to ACL 2023 Questions","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/ACL-2023-session/"},{"title":"Authors Guidelines","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/authors"},{"title":"Incentives 2026","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/incentives2026"},{"title":"ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/"},{"title":"ARR Changelog","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/changelog"},{"title":"ARR SAC Guidelines","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/sacguidelines"},{"title":"Stats","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/stats/"}]},"Based on the search results, I found information about the ACL Rolling Review (ARR) review form scales for soundness, excitement, and overall score:\n\n## Soundness Scale\n\nThe soundness scores include: 5 = Excellent (one of the most thorough studies given its type), 4 = Strong (provides sufficient support for all claims), and 3 = Acceptable (provides sufficient support for main claims).\n\n## Excitement Scale\n\nThe excitement scale ranges from 5 = Highly Exciting (would recommend the paper to others and/or attend its presentation) to 4 = Exciting (would mention the paper to others and/or make an effort to attend), down to 1 = Not Exciting (does not resonate and wouldn't resonate with the *ACL community).\n\nExcitement is subjective and does not necessarily follow what is popular in the field; reviewers may perceive papers as transformational/innovative/surprising because they present conceptual breakthroughs or challenge common assumptions, or because of their potential impact on communities through lowering barriers and enabling new applications.\n\n## Overall Score\n\nStarting with February 2025, ARR reviewers are asked to provide three scores: soundness, excitement, and overall recommendation. The Overall assessment score is an explicit recommendation for the outcome of this paper, if it were committed to an *ACL venue, and is a composite score reflecting your assessment of soundness, excitement, and also other factors like novelty and impact."],"durationSeconds":6.847440019000001}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01V2dN1Pq6HuydWfHkqdZAYe","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have a good picture of the ARR review form. Let me also fetch the exact review form page for the precise scale descriptions.\n\nLet me now proceed with the full review. I have:\n\n1. **ARR Review Form** (used by EMNLP 2026):\n   - **Soundness**: 1-5 scale (5=Excellent, 4=Strong, 3=Acceptable, 2=Poor, 1=Major issues)\n   - **Excitement**: 1-5 scale (5=Highly Exciting, 4=Exciting, 3=Interesting, 2=Marginally interesting, 1=Not Exciting)\n   - **Overall Assessment**: 1-5 scale (recommendation for acceptance)\n\n2. **Paper**: \"Strong Predictors, Weak Selectors: Diagnosing Why Confidence Signals Cannot Scale Structured Information Extraction\" — a 6-page paper with appendix that evaluates five confidence signals for quality estimation and selection in structured IE across SciERC, CoNLL-2003, Few-NERD, and WNUT-17, with two main contributions around a QE framework and identifying a capability trap, tested on Qwen3-8B and LLaMA-3.1-8B.\n\n3. I'm looking at the review context with the planned experiments, then I'll bring in two reviewers in parallel — one focusing on methods and experiments, the other on novelty and related work — before running through a four-step roundtable discussion.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":749,"cache_read_input_tokens":51591,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":749},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2dbb1532-cce8-453c-a0ba-ff5d2a8428c3","uuid":"9848db44-750d-4047-8631-a2d25f9ea6cf"}
