{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01V2dN1Pq6HuydWfHkqdZAYe","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_012j9aktZf9LgbHXUUEJq1rH","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work Review","prompt":"You are Reviewer R2 for an EMNLP 2026 review. Your focus is **novelty and related work**. \n\n## Venue Scoring (ARR 2025+ format)\n- **Soundness** (1-5): 5=Excellent, 4=Strong, 3=Acceptable, 2=Poor, 1=Major issues\n- **Excitement** (1-5): 5=Highly Exciting, 4=Exciting, 3=Interesting, 2=Marginally interesting, 1=Not Exciting\n- **Overall** (1-5): 5=Strong accept, 4=Accept, 3=Borderline, 2=Reject, 1=Strong reject\n- EMNLP acceptance rate ~24%\n\n## Paper Summary\n**Title**: \"Strong Predictors, Weak Selectors: Diagnosing Why Confidence Signals Cannot Scale Structured Information Extraction\"\n\nThe paper systematically evaluates 5 confidence signals across 4 families for quality estimation (QE) and candidate selection in structured IE (NER + RE). Two co-primary contributions:\n\n**Contribution 1 (QE Framework)**: First systematic comparison of 5 confidence signals (Structural Jaccard, Fuzzy token overlap, Exact Match, Voting Confidence, Log-Probability) spanning 4 families for structured IE quality estimation. Finds structural consistency signals outperform LP for NER (ρ=0.38 vs 0.21, Fisher p=0.028), while ranking reverses for RE (LP>SJ, p=0.085, not significant across all seeds). The RE reversal is attributed to a \"binary dominance mechanism\" where 43-45% of RE instances have F1=0.\n\n**Contribution 2 (Failure Diagnosis + Capability Trap)**: Diagnoses why signals that predict quality fail at selection. Two mechanisms:\n- Instance degeneracy: 21-58% of instances produce identical F1 across N=8 samples\n- Log-prob compression: within-instance LP range collapses to 0.041 nats median\n- Unified by \"capability trap\": higher capability → more convergence → more degeneracy → less headroom\n- Fine-tuning intensity is secondary factor (~78% within-dataset, but capability dominates cross-dataset)\n\n**Related Work Section** covers:\n1. Test-Time Scaling (self-consistency, CISC, SelfCertainty, ReASC) — all target reasoning, none address structured IE\n2. Consistency for Structured IE — Nédéllec & Xia study consistency-accuracy correlation with surface-level matching; this paper uses structural Jaccard\n3. Uncertainty Quantification — semantic entropy, conformal prediction, MC-dropout NER uncertainty, CoCoA\n4. MBR Decoding — Eikema et al. diagnose utility misalignment; this paper's failure mode is distinct (utility aligned but headroom eliminated)\n\n**Experimental scope**: 4 datasets (SciERC, CoNLL-2003, Few-NERD, WNUT-17), 3 model configs (Qwen3-8B, LLaMA-3.1-8B, Qwen3-4B), LoRA rank ablation, free-form decoding ablation, 3 seeds.\n\n**Key claim differentiation from MBR**: \"We reveal a qualitatively distinct failure mode: the utility function is aligned (within-instance ρ=0.524 between signal and quality), but selection headroom is eliminated by output convergence.\"\n\n**Paper is 6 pages main content + 1 page appendix.**\n\n## Your Task\nProvide a detailed review focusing on:\n1. **Novelty**: How novel are the contributions? Is the \"capability trap\" concept genuinely new or a restatement of known phenomena? Is the QE framework contribution incremental?\n2. **Related work completeness**: Are important baselines and prior work missing? Is the positioning accurate?\n3. **Significance**: Would EMNLP attendees find this interesting? Does it advance the field meaningfully?\n4. **Clarity of contribution**: Are the contributions clearly delineated and well-motivated?\n5. **Framing and claims**: Are the claims appropriately calibrated? Overclaims? Underclaims?\n\nConsider these specific angles:\n- Self-consistency (Wang et al.) is well-known for reasoning. How novel is it to show it doesn't work for structured IE? Is \"structured outputs don't aggregate like discrete answers\" somewhat obvious?\n- The \"capability trap\" — is this a genuine conceptual contribution or an expected correlation (better models make fewer mistakes, so outputs are more similar)?\n- MBR decoding literature — is the distinction from Eikema et al. genuinely \"qualitatively distinct\" or a matter of degree?\n- Are there missing comparisons to trained rerankers, verifier-based approaches, or other structured prediction scaling methods?\n- The QE framework — how does this compare to existing uncertainty estimation literature for NER/RE?\n\nOutput format:\n```\n## R2 Review: Novelty & Related Work\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Weaknesses\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Missing Related Work / Positioning Issues\n[If any]\n\n### Preliminary Scores\n- Soundness: X/5\n- Excitement: X/5\n- Overall: X/5\n- Confidence: X/5\n\n### Justification\n[Brief justification for scores]\n```\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":749,"cache_read_input_tokens":51591,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":749},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2dbb1532-cce8-453c-a0ba-ff5d2a8428c3","uuid":"93b542e0-ac98-479a-bb6d-8ce6cea28cb0"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"aceeb0dba185339b7","tool_use_id":"toolu_012j9aktZf9LgbHXUUEJq1rH","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work Review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer R2 for an EMNLP 2026 review. Your focus is **novelty and related work**. \n\n## Venue Scoring (ARR 2025+ format)\n- **Soundness** (1-5): 5=Excellent, 4=Strong, 3=Acceptable, 2=Poor, 1=Major issues\n- **Excitement** (1-5): 5=Highly Exciting, 4=Exciting, 3=Interesting, 2=Marginally interesting, 1=Not Exciting\n- **Overall** (1-5): 5=Strong accept, 4=Accept, 3=Borderline, 2=Reject, 1=Strong reject\n- EMNLP acceptance rate ~24%\n\n## Paper Summary\n**Title**: \"Strong Predictors, Weak Selectors: Diagnosing Why Confidence Signals Cannot Scale Structured Information Extraction\"\n\nThe paper systematically evaluates 5 confidence signals across 4 families for quality estimation (QE) and candidate selection in structured IE (NER + RE). Two co-primary contributions:\n\n**Contribution 1 (QE Framework)**: First systematic comparison of 5 confidence signals (Structural Jaccard, Fuzzy token overlap, Exact Match, Voting Confidence, Log-Probability) spanning 4 families for structured IE quality estimation. Finds structural consistency signals outperform LP for NER (ρ=0.38 vs 0.21, Fisher p=0.028), while ranking reverses for RE (LP>SJ, p=0.085, not significant across all seeds). The RE reversal is attributed to a \"binary dominance mechanism\" where 43-45% of RE instances have F1=0.\n\n**Contribution 2 (Failure Diagnosis + Capability Trap)**: Diagnoses why signals that predict quality fail at selection. Two mechanisms:\n- Instance degeneracy: 21-58% of instances produce identical F1 across N=8 samples\n- Log-prob compression: within-instance LP range collapses to 0.041 nats median\n- Unified by \"capability trap\": higher capability → more convergence → more degeneracy → less headroom\n- Fine-tuning intensity is secondary factor (~78% within-dataset, but capability dominates cross-dataset)\n\n**Related Work Section** covers:\n1. Test-Time Scaling (self-consistency, CISC, SelfCertainty, ReASC) — all target reasoning, none address structured IE\n2. Consistency for Structured IE — Nédéllec & Xia study consistency-accuracy correlation with surface-level matching; this paper uses structural Jaccard\n3. Uncertainty Quantification — semantic entropy, conformal prediction, MC-dropout NER uncertainty, CoCoA\n4. MBR Decoding — Eikema et al. diagnose utility misalignment; this paper's failure mode is distinct (utility aligned but headroom eliminated)\n\n**Experimental scope**: 4 datasets (SciERC, CoNLL-2003, Few-NERD, WNUT-17), 3 model configs (Qwen3-8B, LLaMA-3.1-8B, Qwen3-4B), LoRA rank ablation, free-form decoding ablation, 3 seeds.\n\n**Key claim differentiation from MBR**: \"We reveal a qualitatively distinct failure mode: the utility function is aligned (within-instance ρ=0.524 between signal and quality), but selection headroom is eliminated by output convergence.\"\n\n**Paper is 6 pages main content + 1 page appendix.**\n\n## Your Task\nProvide a detailed review focusing on:\n1. **Novelty**: How novel are the contributions? Is the \"capability trap\" concept genuinely new or a restatement of known phenomena? Is the QE framework contribution incremental?\n2. **Related work completeness**: Are important baselines and prior work missing? Is the positioning accurate?\n3. **Significance**: Would EMNLP attendees find this interesting? Does it advance the field meaningfully?\n4. **Clarity of contribution**: Are the contributions clearly delineated and well-motivated?\n5. **Framing and claims**: Are the claims appropriately calibrated? Overclaims? Underclaims?\n\nConsider these specific angles:\n- Self-consistency (Wang et al.) is well-known for reasoning. How novel is it to show it doesn't work for structured IE? Is \"structured outputs don't aggregate like discrete answers\" somewhat obvious?\n- The \"capability trap\" — is this a genuine conceptual contribution or an expected correlation (better models make fewer mistakes, so outputs are more similar)?\n- MBR decoding literature — is the distinction from Eikema et al. genuinely \"qualitatively distinct\" or a matter of degree?\n- Are there missing comparisons to trained rerankers, verifier-based approaches, or other structured prediction scaling methods?\n- The QE framework — how does this compare to existing uncertainty estimation literature for NER/RE?\n\nOutput format:\n```\n## R2 Review: Novelty & Related Work\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Weaknesses\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Missing Related Work / Positioning Issues\n[If any]\n\n### Preliminary Scores\n- Soundness: X/5\n- Excitement: X/5\n- Overall: X/5\n- Confidence: X/5\n\n### Justification\n[Brief justification for scores]\n```\n","uuid":"b17dafc1-b763-4fca-bae9-80bfd083603d","session_id":"2dbb1532-cce8-453c-a0ba-ff5d2a8428c3"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer R2 for an EMNLP 2026 review. Your focus is **novelty and related work**. \n\n## Venue Scoring (ARR 2025+ format)\n- **Soundness** (1-5): 5=Excellent, 4=Strong, 3=Acceptable, 2=Poor, 1=Major issues\n- **Excitement** (1-5): 5=Highly Exciting, 4=Exciting, 3=Interesting, 2=Marginally interesting, 1=Not Exciting\n- **Overall** (1-5): 5=Strong accept, 4=Accept, 3=Borderline, 2=Reject, 1=Strong reject\n- EMNLP acceptance rate ~24%\n\n## Paper Summary\n**Title**: \"Strong Predictors, Weak Selectors: Diagnosing Why Confidence Signals Cannot Scale Structured Information Extraction\"\n\nThe paper systematically evaluates 5 confidence signals across 4 families for quality estimation (QE) and candidate selection in structured IE (NER + RE). Two co-primary contributions:\n\n**Contribution 1 (QE Framework)**: First systematic comparison of 5 confidence signals (Structural Jaccard, Fuzzy token overlap, Exact Match, Voting Confidence, Log-Probability) spanning 4 families for structured IE quality estimation. Finds structural consistency signals outperform LP for NER (ρ=0.38 vs 0.21, Fisher p=0.028), while ranking reverses for RE (LP>SJ, p=0.085, not significant across all seeds). The RE reversal is attributed to a \"binary dominance mechanism\" where 43-45% of RE instances have F1=0.\n\n**Contribution 2 (Failure Diagnosis + Capability Trap)**: Diagnoses why signals that predict quality fail at selection. Two mechanisms:\n- Instance degeneracy: 21-58% of instances produce identical F1 across N=8 samples\n- Log-prob compression: within-instance LP range collapses to 0.041 nats median\n- Unified by \"capability trap\": higher capability → more convergence → more degeneracy → less headroom\n- Fine-tuning intensity is secondary factor (~78% within-dataset, but capability dominates cross-dataset)\n\n**Related Work Section** covers:\n1. Test-Time Scaling (self-consistency, CISC, SelfCertainty, ReASC) — all target reasoning, none address structured IE\n2. Consistency for Structured IE — Nédéllec & Xia study consistency-accuracy correlation with surface-level matching; this paper uses structural Jaccard\n3. Uncertainty Quantification — semantic entropy, conformal prediction, MC-dropout NER uncertainty, CoCoA\n4. MBR Decoding — Eikema et al. diagnose utility misalignment; this paper's failure mode is distinct (utility aligned but headroom eliminated)\n\n**Experimental scope**: 4 datasets (SciERC, CoNLL-2003, Few-NERD, WNUT-17), 3 model configs (Qwen3-8B, LLaMA-3.1-8B, Qwen3-4B), LoRA rank ablation, free-form decoding ablation, 3 seeds.\n\n**Key claim differentiation from MBR**: \"We reveal a qualitatively distinct failure mode: the utility function is aligned (within-instance ρ=0.524 between signal and quality), but selection headroom is eliminated by output convergence.\"\n\n**Paper is 6 pages main content + 1 page appendix.**\n\n## Your Task\nProvide a detailed review focusing on:\n1. **Novelty**: How novel are the contributions? Is the \"capability trap\" concept genuinely new or a restatement of known phenomena? Is the QE framework contribution incremental?\n2. **Related work completeness**: Are important baselines and prior work missing? Is the positioning accurate?\n3. **Significance**: Would EMNLP attendees find this interesting? Does it advance the field meaningfully?\n4. **Clarity of contribution**: Are the contributions clearly delineated and well-motivated?\n5. **Framing and claims**: Are the claims appropriately calibrated? Overclaims? Underclaims?\n\nConsider these specific angles:\n- Self-consistency (Wang et al.) is well-known for reasoning. How novel is it to show it doesn't work for structured IE? Is \"structured outputs don't aggregate like discrete answers\" somewhat obvious?\n- The \"capability trap\" — is this a genuine conceptual contribution or an expected correlation (better models make fewer mistakes, so outputs are more similar)?\n- MBR decoding literature — is the distinction from Eikema et al. genuinely \"qualitatively distinct\" or a matter of degree?\n- Are there missing comparisons to trained rerankers, verifier-based approaches, or other structured prediction scaling methods?\n- The QE framework — how does this compare to existing uncertainty estimation literature for NER/RE?\n\nOutput format:\n```\n## R2 Review: Novelty & Related Work\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Weaknesses\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n[Numbered list]\n\n### Missing Related Work / Positioning Issues\n[If any]\n\n### Preliminary Scores\n- Soundness: X/5\n- Excitement: X/5\n- Overall: X/5\n- Confidence: X/5\n\n### Justification\n[Brief justification for scores]\n```\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_012j9aktZf9LgbHXUUEJq1rH","session_id":"2dbb1532-cce8-453c-a0ba-ff5d2a8428c3","uuid":"a600bb3b-c0bd-476e-b670-f6ce9698165a","timestamp":"2026-05-16T10:18:17.956Z"}
