{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01V8xtdaBiSAFR6paKaA2YeF","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_019PymFo6G8fx57fHieCz2U3","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0514_1/projects/struct_self_consist_ie/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260516T092241/best/paper.tex","limit":50},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2732,"cache_read_input_tokens":54966,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2732,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_012j9aktZf9LgbHXUUEJq1rH","session_id":"2dbb1532-cce8-453c-a0ba-ff5d2a8428c3","uuid":"8445dcdd-12e6-4ea4-8361-c9b10e3a2570"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019PymFo6G8fx57fHieCz2U3","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt,twocolumn]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n3\t\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n4\t\\usepackage{times}\n5\t\\usepackage{geometry}\n6\t\\geometry{a4paper,margin=2.5cm}\n7\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n8\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n9\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n10\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n11\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n12\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n13\t\\usepackage{tablefootnote}\n14\t\\usepackage{natbib}\n15\t\\bibliographystyle{plainnat}\n16\t\\usepackage{caption}\n17\t\\captionsetup{font=small}\n18\t\n19\t\\title{Strong Predictors, Weak Selectors:\\\\Why Confidence Signals Cannot Scale\\\\Structured Information Extraction}\n20\t\n21\t\\author{Anonymous EMNLP 2026 Submission}\n22\t\\date{}\n23\t\n24\t\\begin{document}\n25\t\\maketitle\n26\t\n27\t% ============================================================\n28\t\\begin{abstract}\n29\t% ============================================================\n30\tTest-time scaling via multi-sample generation improves LLM performance on reasoning tasks, but its effectiveness for structured information extraction (IE) remains unclear.\n31\tWe systematically evaluate five confidence signals across four families for quality estimation and best-of-$N$ selection on three NER datasets (CoNLL-2003, SciERC, Few-NERD) and one RE dataset (SciERC), using Qwen3-8B, Qwen3-4B, and LLaMA-3.1-8B.\n32\tWhile confidence signals reliably predict output quality (AUROC up to 0.82 on CoNLL, Spearman $\\rho$ up to 0.45), they \\emph{universally fail} to select better candidates than greedy ($T{=}0$) decoding.\n33\tWe diagnose two failure mechanisms: (1)~\\textbf{instance degeneracy}---21--58\\% of instances produce identical outputs across all $N$ samples, eliminating selection headroom; and (2)~\\textbf{logprob compression}---the only per-sample signal exhibits within-instance score ranges of merely 0.028--0.041 nats, rendering candidate discrimination impossible.\n34\tThese mechanisms are unified by a \\textbf{capability trap}: as model capability increases, outputs converge, degeneracy rises, and selection headroom vanishes.\n35\tWe confirm this pattern across three NER datasets ($R^2{=}0.94$ for the F1--degeneracy relationship), two model scales, and a LoRA rank ablation that rules out fine-tuning intensity as a confound.\n36\tOur results establish that $T{=}0$ greedy decoding dominates all multi-sample strategies for structured IE, and that closing the gap requires fundamentally different approaches such as trained verifiers.\n37\t\\end{abstract}\n38\t\n39\t% ============================================================\n40\t\\section{Introduction}\n41\t\\label{sec:intro}\n42\t% ============================================================\n43\t\n44\tTest-time scaling (TTS)---generating multiple candidate outputs and selecting the best one---has emerged as a powerful strategy for improving LLM performance \\citep{wang2023selfconsistency,snell2024scaling,brown2024large}.\n45\tSelf-consistency \\citep{wang2023selfconsistency} and minimum Bayes risk (MBR) decoding \\citep{eikema2022sampling} demonstrate that multi-sample strategies can substantially outperform single-sample greedy decoding on reasoning and translation tasks.\n46\t\n47\tStructured information extraction (IE)---extracting typed entity sets and relational triples from text---presents a fundamentally different challenge.\n48\tUnlike multiple-choice reasoning where answers are discrete tokens, IE outputs are \\emph{sets of structured objects} with partial credit: two candidate entity sets may share some entities but differ on others.\n49\tThis structural richness suggests that consistency-based confidence signals could be particularly informative, as they can measure agreement at multiple granularities (exact match, entity overlap, structural similarity).\n50\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_012j9aktZf9LgbHXUUEJq1rH","session_id":"2dbb1532-cce8-453c-a0ba-ff5d2a8428c3","uuid":"4455ea7d-b141-4849-a2e0-8f1a10b75672","timestamp":"2026-05-16T10:18:56.646Z"}
