{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01FMedniup3Pgy26CiWoSrc8","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01NnhvuavkVBUzsNn2rvfjj7","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1 responds to R2's review","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 (Methods & Experiments focus) in a roundtable discussion for an EMNLP 2026 review of \"Strong Predictors, Weak Selectors: Diagnosing Why Confidence Signals Cannot Scale Structured Information Extraction.\"\n\n## Your Original Review (R1)\nYour key points were:\n- Soundness: 3/5, Excitement: 3/5, Overall: 3/5\n- **Strengths**: Well-designed QE/selection separation, rigorous statistical analysis (bootstrap, Fisher, TOST), thorough ablation chain, honest negative result reporting, multi-seed stability, difficulty gradient design.\n- **Key weaknesses**: (1) Narrow model scale 4B-8B only, (2) possible LoRA hyperparameter discrepancy, (3) only 2 seeds for primary selection, (4) RE results are exploratory/single-dataset, (5) N=8 may be too small, (6) missing threshold-based voting alternatives.\n- **Missing experiments**: Larger N investigation, cross-domain transfer, few-shot vs fine-tuned comparison.\n\n## R2's Review (Novelty & Related Work)\nR2 gave: Soundness 4/5, Excitement 2/5, Overall 2/5.\n\n**R2's Strengths**: Thorough related work, explicit MBR positioning, systematic signal taxonomy, ablation rigor, honest CoCoA/verifier positioning.\n\n**R2's Key Weaknesses**:\n1. \"Capability trap\" is arguably tautological — \"better models produce more deterministic outputs\" is close to a truism. R² between F1 and degeneracy essentially says \"easy tasks have less variance.\"\n2. Core negative finding has limited novelty — fine-tuned NER models converging is the EXPECTED outcome. Paper may be refuting a claim nobody makes.\n3. QE framework is incremental — standard components, intuitive findings.\n4. Missing reranking/verifier comparison — even simple supervised baseline would ground \"how much is recoverable.\"\n5. MBR distinction may be overstated as \"qualitatively distinct.\"\n6. Narrow model scale.\n\n**R2's Questions**:\n- Is there evidence practitioners expect multi-sample to work for IE?\n- 4B shows WORSE selection (-1.49pp) despite lower degeneracy — contradicts capability trap?\n- How is degeneracy different from known \"sharp distribution\" phenomenon in MBR?\n- Free-form helps RE but not NER — is the trap task-specific?\n\n**R2's Missing Work**: Best-of-N with reward models for structured tasks, calibration-aware selection, diverse decoding strategies (nucleus, typical sampling), selective prediction literature, concurrent TTS limitations work.\n\n## Your Task\nRespond to R2's review. For each of R2's major points:\n1. Do you agree or disagree? Why?\n2. Has R2's review changed any of your scores or assessment?\n3. Are there points R2 raised that you missed?\n\nAlso note: Where do you and R2 converge? Where do you fundamentally disagree?\n\nKeep your response focused and under 600 words.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8984,"cache_read_input_tokens":52340,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":8984},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2dbb1532-cce8-453c-a0ba-ff5d2a8428c3","uuid":"a7a85828-6054-43b9-bb08-281395083b6a"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"a2dc3faab42056219","tool_use_id":"toolu_01NnhvuavkVBUzsNn2rvfjj7","description":"R1 responds to R2's review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 (Methods & Experiments focus) in a roundtable discussion for an EMNLP 2026 review of \"Strong Predictors, Weak Selectors: Diagnosing Why Confidence Signals Cannot Scale Structured Information Extraction.\"\n\n## Your Original Review (R1)\nYour key points were:\n- Soundness: 3/5, Excitement: 3/5, Overall: 3/5\n- **Strengths**: Well-designed QE/selection separation, rigorous statistical analysis (bootstrap, Fisher, TOST), thorough ablation chain, honest negative result reporting, multi-seed stability, difficulty gradient design.\n- **Key weaknesses**: (1) Narrow model scale 4B-8B only, (2) possible LoRA hyperparameter discrepancy, (3) only 2 seeds for primary selection, (4) RE results are exploratory/single-dataset, (5) N=8 may be too small, (6) missing threshold-based voting alternatives.\n- **Missing experiments**: Larger N investigation, cross-domain transfer, few-shot vs fine-tuned comparison.\n\n## R2's Review (Novelty & Related Work)\nR2 gave: Soundness 4/5, Excitement 2/5, Overall 2/5.\n\n**R2's Strengths**: Thorough related work, explicit MBR positioning, systematic signal taxonomy, ablation rigor, honest CoCoA/verifier positioning.\n\n**R2's Key Weaknesses**:\n1. \"Capability trap\" is arguably tautological — \"better models produce more deterministic outputs\" is close to a truism. R² between F1 and degeneracy essentially says \"easy tasks have less variance.\"\n2. Core negative finding has limited novelty — fine-tuned NER models converging is the EXPECTED outcome. Paper may be refuting a claim nobody makes.\n3. QE framework is incremental — standard components, intuitive findings.\n4. Missing reranking/verifier comparison — even simple supervised baseline would ground \"how much is recoverable.\"\n5. MBR distinction may be overstated as \"qualitatively distinct.\"\n6. Narrow model scale.\n\n**R2's Questions**:\n- Is there evidence practitioners expect multi-sample to work for IE?\n- 4B shows WORSE selection (-1.49pp) despite lower degeneracy — contradicts capability trap?\n- How is degeneracy different from known \"sharp distribution\" phenomenon in MBR?\n- Free-form helps RE but not NER — is the trap task-specific?\n\n**R2's Missing Work**: Best-of-N with reward models for structured tasks, calibration-aware selection, diverse decoding strategies (nucleus, typical sampling), selective prediction literature, concurrent TTS limitations work.\n\n## Your Task\nRespond to R2's review. For each of R2's major points:\n1. Do you agree or disagree? Why?\n2. Has R2's review changed any of your scores or assessment?\n3. Are there points R2 raised that you missed?\n\nAlso note: Where do you and R2 converge? Where do you fundamentally disagree?\n\nKeep your response focused and under 600 words.\n","uuid":"4e411ac6-4c56-4ad2-9171-74439821b750","session_id":"2dbb1532-cce8-453c-a0ba-ff5d2a8428c3"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer R1 (Methods & Experiments focus) in a roundtable discussion for an EMNLP 2026 review of \"Strong Predictors, Weak Selectors: Diagnosing Why Confidence Signals Cannot Scale Structured Information Extraction.\"\n\n## Your Original Review (R1)\nYour key points were:\n- Soundness: 3/5, Excitement: 3/5, Overall: 3/5\n- **Strengths**: Well-designed QE/selection separation, rigorous statistical analysis (bootstrap, Fisher, TOST), thorough ablation chain, honest negative result reporting, multi-seed stability, difficulty gradient design.\n- **Key weaknesses**: (1) Narrow model scale 4B-8B only, (2) possible LoRA hyperparameter discrepancy, (3) only 2 seeds for primary selection, (4) RE results are exploratory/single-dataset, (5) N=8 may be too small, (6) missing threshold-based voting alternatives.\n- **Missing experiments**: Larger N investigation, cross-domain transfer, few-shot vs fine-tuned comparison.\n\n## R2's Review (Novelty & Related Work)\nR2 gave: Soundness 4/5, Excitement 2/5, Overall 2/5.\n\n**R2's Strengths**: Thorough related work, explicit MBR positioning, systematic signal taxonomy, ablation rigor, honest CoCoA/verifier positioning.\n\n**R2's Key Weaknesses**:\n1. \"Capability trap\" is arguably tautological — \"better models produce more deterministic outputs\" is close to a truism. R² between F1 and degeneracy essentially says \"easy tasks have less variance.\"\n2. Core negative finding has limited novelty — fine-tuned NER models converging is the EXPECTED outcome. Paper may be refuting a claim nobody makes.\n3. QE framework is incremental — standard components, intuitive findings.\n4. Missing reranking/verifier comparison — even simple supervised baseline would ground \"how much is recoverable.\"\n5. MBR distinction may be overstated as \"qualitatively distinct.\"\n6. Narrow model scale.\n\n**R2's Questions**:\n- Is there evidence practitioners expect multi-sample to work for IE?\n- 4B shows WORSE selection (-1.49pp) despite lower degeneracy — contradicts capability trap?\n- How is degeneracy different from known \"sharp distribution\" phenomenon in MBR?\n- Free-form helps RE but not NER — is the trap task-specific?\n\n**R2's Missing Work**: Best-of-N with reward models for structured tasks, calibration-aware selection, diverse decoding strategies (nucleus, typical sampling), selective prediction literature, concurrent TTS limitations work.\n\n## Your Task\nRespond to R2's review. For each of R2's major points:\n1. Do you agree or disagree? Why?\n2. Has R2's review changed any of your scores or assessment?\n3. Are there points R2 raised that you missed?\n\nAlso note: Where do you and R2 converge? Where do you fundamentally disagree?\n\nKeep your response focused and under 600 words.\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01NnhvuavkVBUzsNn2rvfjj7","session_id":"2dbb1532-cce8-453c-a0ba-ff5d2a8428c3","uuid":"dacb9509-cde4-4e9d-b506-2dfb2d25058f","timestamp":"2026-05-16T10:20:36.611Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01FMedniup3Pgy26CiWoSrc8","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01DSwhSHzW3Y4uQkV3AeXcia","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2 responds to R1's review","prompt":"You are Reviewer R2 (Novelty & Related Work focus) in a roundtable discussion for an EMNLP 2026 review of \"Strong Predictors, Weak Selectors: Diagnosing Why Confidence Signals Cannot Scale Structured Information Extraction.\"\n\n## Your Original Review (R2)\nYour key points were:\n- Soundness: 4/5, Excitement: 2/5, Overall: 2/5\n- **Strengths**: Thorough related work, explicit MBR positioning, systematic signal taxonomy, ablation rigor.\n- **Key weaknesses**: (1) \"Capability trap\" is tautological, (2) negative finding has limited novelty, (3) QE framework is incremental, (4) missing verifier comparison, (5) MBR distinction overstated, (6) narrow scale.\n- Core concern: The paper refutes something partially obvious (self-consistency doesn't help fine-tuned IE), lacks a positive contribution (no working remedy), and the \"capability trap\" concept reduces to \"good models are deterministic.\"\n\n## R1's Review (Methods & Experiments)\nR1 gave: Soundness 3/5, Excitement 3/5, Overall 3/5.\n\n**R1's Strengths**: Well-designed evaluation framework separating QE from selection, rigorous statistical analysis (bootstrap CIs, Fisher combined p-values, TOST, Cohen's d), thorough 5-ablation chain, honest negative result reporting, multi-seed stability, difficulty gradient design.\n\n**R1's Key Weaknesses**:\n1. Narrow model scale (4B-8B only) — capability trap claim extrapolates beyond evidence.\n2. LoRA hyperparameter discrepancy (rank=32 in paper vs rank=16 in summary).\n3. Only 2 seeds for primary selection results — underpowered for the central claim.\n4. Few-NERD and LoRA rank ablation may be missing from actual manuscript.\n5. RE is single-dataset and exploratory.\n6. Possible micro/macro F1 mixing bug in Table 2.\n7. N=8 may be too small — degeneracy could be small-N artifact.\n8. Missing threshold-based voting alternatives.\n\n**R1's Questions**:\n- Has the Table 2 micro/macro F1 inconsistency been resolved?\n- Can capability be disentangled from LoRA memorization?\n- Why not test threshold-based majority voting?\n- Is the epsilon=0.05 threshold for non-tied LP subset post-hoc?\n- Does degeneracy decrease substantially at N=32/64?\n\n**R1's Missing Experiments**: Third productive NER dataset, larger N investigation, LoRA rank ablation in main paper, cross-domain transfer, few-shot vs fine-tuned comparison.\n\n## Your Task\nRespond to R1's review. For each of R1's major points:\n1. Do you agree or disagree? Why?\n2. Has R1's review changed any of your scores or assessment?\n3. Are there points R1 raised that you missed?\n\nAlso note: Where do you and R1 converge? Where do you fundamentally disagree?\n\nKeep your response focused and under 600 words.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8984,"cache_read_input_tokens":52340,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":8984},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2dbb1532-cce8-453c-a0ba-ff5d2a8428c3","uuid":"adf5a3cf-ebf8-40e5-bf1f-b2b5933c8aa8"}
