{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"toolu_01Cgj2SHZSTZGt33VRh7ACLw","content":[{"type":"tool_reference","tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2dbb1532-cce8-453c-a0ba-ff5d2a8428c3","uuid":"d025c334-5ec2-47c7-9462-3ef548df1937","timestamp":"2026-05-16T10:22:20.342Z","tool_use_result":{"matches":["mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report"],"query":"select:mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","total_deferred_tools":23}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01AfSDFDN5GB9W59fcty2Nva","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_019i8Jm5fwxhuURZGsZC6G1W","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"report":"# Dream Review Report: Strong Predictors, Weak Selectors\n\n**Paper**: \"Strong Predictors, Weak Selectors: Diagnosing Why Confidence Signals Cannot Scale Structured Information Extraction\"\n**Venue**: EMNLP 2026 Main Conference (ARR 2025+ review form)\n**Review Mode**: Full (2-reviewer roundtable, 4-step protocol)\n**Date**: 2026-05-16\n\n---\n\n## Venue Rubric (ARR 2025+)\n\n- **Soundness** (1–5): Technical correctness and experimental rigor\n- **Excitement** (1–5): Novelty, significance, impact potential\n- **Overall** (1–5): 5=Strong Accept, 4=Accept, 3=Borderline, 2=Reject, 1=Strong Reject\n- EMNLP acceptance rate: ~24%\n\n---\n\n## Paper Summary\n\nThe paper systematically evaluates 5 confidence signals across 4 families (structural agreement, surface similarity, generation probability) for quality estimation (QE) and candidate selection in structured IE (NER + RE). Two co-primary contributions: (1) a QE framework showing structural consistency signals outperform log-probability for NER (ρ=0.38 vs 0.21, Fisher p=0.028), and (2) a diagnosis of why signals that predict quality fail at selection—two mechanisms (instance degeneracy, log-prob compression) unified by a \"capability trap\" where higher model capability drives output convergence. Tested on 4 datasets (SciERC, CoNLL-2003, Few-NERD, WNUT-17), 3 model configurations (Qwen3-8B, LLaMA-3.1-8B, Qwen3-4B), with LoRA rank ablation and free-form decoding ablation.\n\n## Experiment Coverage vs. Design\n\nThe review context YAML lists 5 imagined experiments: (1) fewnerd_smoke, (2) fewnerd_full, (3) 3rd_seed_scierc, (4) lora_rank8_ablation, (5) claim_audit. All 5 are reflected in the paper (Few-NERD in main results + Appendix A, 3rd seed in Fisher combined tests, LoRA ablation in Section 5.5, claim audit in Section 5.7). Coverage is complete.\n\n---\n\n## R1 Review: Methods & Experiments\n\n### Strengths\n\n1. **Well-designed QE/selection separation.** The distinction between instance-level signals (which cannot rank candidates by construction) and per-sample signals (LP) is clearly articulated and drives the experimental design. Non-trivial methodological contribution.\n\n2. **Rigorous statistical analysis.** Bootstrap CIs for signal comparisons, Fisher combined p-values across seeds, Cohen's d for effect sizes, TOST equivalence testing. The paper reports non-significant p-values honestly (p=0.39 for SciERC selection, p=0.42 for Few-NERD).\n\n3. **Thorough ablation chain.** Five ablations (free-form decoding, LoRA rank, model scale, cross-architecture, non-degenerate subset) systematically eliminate confounds. The free-form ablation (LP std ratio 0.97) convincingly rules out constrained decoding as the cause of LP compression.\n\n4. **Honest negative result reporting.** Claims are appropriately hedged (\"predominantly fail\" not \"universally fail\"). Effect sizes are small and reported as such.\n\n5. **Multi-seed stability.** Three seeds with SD ≤ 0.032 for SJ ρ. Signal rankings are consistent across seeds.\n\n6. **Difficulty gradient design.** Four datasets spanning F1=0.52 to F1=0.91 systematically map the capability-degeneracy relationship.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n1. **Narrow model scale (4B–8B only).** The \"capability trap\" claim extrapolates beyond a 2× parameter range. At 70B+ or frontier models, sampling diversity may behave qualitatively differently. The paper acknowledges this but the abstract and introduction frame findings as general.\n\n2. **4B results contradict the capability trap.** Qwen3-4B shows *worse* selection (−1.49pp vs greedy) despite lower degeneracy (10.4% vs 21%). If lower degeneracy doesn't improve selection, the degeneracy → headroom narrative is incomplete. This anomaly is not adequately discussed.\n\n3. **N=8 may be too small.** For outputs with high combinatorial complexity (sets of typed entities), 8 samples may not adequately explore the output space. The paper does not investigate whether degeneracy decreases substantially at N=32 or N=64.\n\n4. **RE coverage is thin.** Single dataset (SciERC RE), treated as exploratory, with greedy dominating all signals. The title claims \"structured information extraction\" but the paper is primarily a NER study.\n\n5. **Missing threshold-based aggregation alternatives.** The paper tests selection (pick best sample) but not soft aggregation strategies (e.g., include entity if frequency > threshold, entity-level voting with tuned thresholds).\n\n### R1 Scores\n- Soundness: 3/5\n- Excitement: 2/5\n- Overall: 2.5/5\n\n---\n\n## R2 Review: Novelty & Related Work\n\n### Strengths\n\n1. **Thorough related work coverage.** Correctly positions against self-consistency (Wang et al.), CISC, SelfCertainty, ReASC for reasoning; Nédéllec & Xia for IE consistency; semantic entropy, conformal prediction, CoCoA for uncertainty; Eikema et al. for MBR.\n\n2. **Explicit MBR distinction.** \"Utility IS aligned (within-instance ρ=0.524) but headroom eliminated by degeneracy\" is well-articulated and genuinely different from utility misalignment. This is the paper's strongest novelty claim.\n\n3. **Systematic signal taxonomy.** The 4-family, 5-signal decomposition with the instance-level vs per-sample distinction provides clarity for future work.\n\n4. **Appropriate claim calibration.** The paper hedges correctly throughout—LP>SJ for RE is reported as \"tendency\" (Fisher p=0.085), not definitive. The capability trap is framed with R²=0.72, acknowledging residual variance.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n1. **\"Capability trap\" is near-tautological.** \"Better models produce more deterministic outputs, so there's less room for selection\" is close to a truism. A model achieving 91% F1 *should* produce similar outputs across samples. The correlation between F1 and degeneracy essentially says \"easy tasks have less variance,\" well-known in calibration literature. The paper needs to argue why this is surprising in the structured IE context specifically.\n\n2. **Limited novelty of the core negative finding.** Self-consistency works for reasoning because multiple reasoning paths converge to a correct discrete answer. For fine-tuned NER, convergence is the *expected* outcome of successful fine-tuning. No evidence is provided that practitioners or researchers actually expect multi-sample selection to work for fine-tuned structured IE. The paper may be refuting a claim nobody makes.\n\n3. **QE framework is incremental.** Individual components (Jaccard similarity, exact match, voting confidence, log-probability) are all standard. The main finding—structural > surface > probabilistic for NER—is intuitive. Competent engineering, not a conceptual advance.\n\n4. **Missing reranking/verifier comparison.** The paper identifies trained verifiers as the solution but never demonstrates how much oracle gap they could close. Even a simple supervised baseline (linear probe on LP+SJ features) would ground the \"how much is recoverable\" question. Without this, the paper cannot distinguish \"confidence signals are inherently uninformative\" from \"these particular unsupervised signals are weak.\"\n\n5. **MBR distinction may be overstated.** Instance degeneracy and utility misalignment differ in mechanism but not in qualitative consequence. The MBR literature already discusses \"peaky\" distributions where MBR gains vanish. Framing as \"qualitatively novel\" risks overclaim.\n\n6. **Missing related work.** (a) Best-of-N with reward models for structured generation (code generation literature), (b) Calibration-aware selection (Zhao et al. 2021), (c) Diverse decoding strategies (nucleus, typical sampling, diverse beam search), (d) Selective prediction / abstention as a more natural application of the QE signals.\n\n### R2 Scores\n- Soundness: 4/5\n- Excitement: 2/5\n- Overall: 2/5\n\n---\n\n## Roundtable Discussion Summary\n\n### Step 2: Cross-Sharing\n\n**R1 moved toward R2** on the motivation question. R1 initially gave Excitement 3/5 but accepted R2's argument that diagnosing an expected failure without a surprising finding or remedy is insufficient. R1 revised Excitement to 2/5 and Overall to 2.5/5.\n\n**R2 acknowledged R1's catches**: The 4B anomaly (worse selection despite lower degeneracy contradicting the capability trap), possible LoRA hyperparameter discrepancy, and micro/macro F1 mixing concern. R2 maintained scores, noting these issues reinforce the lower assessment.\n\n### Step 3: Convergence\n\n**Consensus strengths:**\n- Statistical methodology is careful and appropriate\n- Signal taxonomy and QE/selection decomposition are clean\n- Related work is thorough, MBR positioning is well-handled\n- Claims are honestly hedged\n- Ablation chain is well-designed\n\n**Consensus weaknesses:**\n- Narrow model scale (4B–8B) limits generality of capability trap\n- Missing trained selector/reranker baselines — cannot distinguish \"signals uninformative\" from \"these heuristics weak\"\n- Motivation gap — no evidence community expects multi-sample selection to work for fine-tuned IE\n- No remedy offered — pure negative result without boundary conditions or constructive alternatives\n- RE coverage is thin (single dataset, exploratory)\n- Core finding borders on tautological (fine-tuned models are near-deterministic)\n\n**Resolved disagreements:**\n- Both converge that the QE framework is incremental rather than a standalone contribution\n- Both converge on Excitement 2/5\n- R1 moved from Overall 3 to 2.5, approaching R2's 2\n\n**Remaining disagreement:**\n- Soundness: R1=3 (flags 4B anomaly, N=8 concern) vs R2=4 (methodology is sound despite these)\n\n---\n\n## Final Converged Scores\n\n| Criterion | Score | Rationale |\n|-----------|-------|-----------|\n| **Soundness** | 3.5/5 | Methodology is careful with appropriate statistical tests, but implementation concerns (4B anomaly contradicting central thesis, narrow scale, N=8 sample size) prevent full marks |\n| **Excitement** | 2/5 | Both reviewers converge: the finding is near-expected, no remedy is offered, and the motivation gap is unresolved |\n| **Overall** | 2/5 | Below acceptance bar for EMNLP Main given limited novelty, narrow scope, and missing baselines |\n| **Confidence** | 4/5 | Deep engagement from both reviewers with cross-validation of reasoning |\n\n## Final Recommendation: **Reject**\n\nThe paper is methodologically competent but addresses a question whose answer is largely predictable. Fine-tuned models producing near-deterministic outputs—making sampling-based selection ineffective—is close to expected behavior. The \"capability trap\" concept, while named evocatively, reduces to \"good models are deterministic,\" a known property. Neither reviewer identifies a finding that would change how practitioners approach structured IE. The narrow model scale (4B–8B) prevents the capability trap from being a general insight, and the absence of trained selector baselines leaves the negative result ambiguous. A well-executed experiment answering a question the community does not actively ask, without offering a constructive path forward, does not meet the EMNLP Main bar.\n\n**Suggested venue**: EMNLP Findings or a workshop paper, with compressed claims focused on the QE signal taxonomy and the non-degenerate subset finding (+2.9pp, p=0.01) as the most interesting result.\n\n---\n\n## Actionable Suggestions for Authors\n\n1. **Scale up.** Include ≥13B models (ideally 70B+) where the capability-but-not-deterministic regime may exist. If the trap persists at scale, the finding becomes genuinely informative.\n\n2. **Add trained baselines.** Compare against a lightweight learned verifier or reranker (even logistic regression on signal features for per-sample ranking). This grounds the claim as \"confidence signals fail\" rather than \"unsupervised heuristics fail.\"\n\n3. **Find the boundary.** Identify conditions where multi-sample selection *does* help (zero-shot, low-resource, out-of-domain, partial fine-tuning). A negative result with a clear boundary condition is far more publishable than a blanket negative.\n\n4. **Establish motivation empirically.** Cite or survey practitioners who use/advocate multi-sample IE strategies. Alternatively, reframe as \"when to stop sampling\" rather than \"why sampling fails.\"\n\n5. **Increase N.** Test N=32 or N=64 to rule out the possibility that degeneracy is a small-N artifact.\n\n6. **Resolve the 4B anomaly.** Qwen3-4B showing worse selection despite lower degeneracy directly contradicts the capability trap. This needs explicit discussion or the framing needs revision.\n\n7. **Expand RE.** Add at least one more RE dataset (TACRED, DocRED) to move beyond exploratory status.\n\n8. **Explore diverse decoding.** Test nucleus sampling, typical sampling, or diverse beam search as alternatives to temperature sampling. If these break degeneracy, the finding is about the sampling strategy, not a fundamental limitation.\n\n9. **Lean into the positive.** The non-degenerate subset result (+2.9pp, p=0.01) and the QE signal taxonomy are the most interesting contributions. Consider making these the primary focus rather than the negative selection finding.\n","project":"struct_self_consist_ie","tier":"median","dream_id":"20260516T092241","overall_score":"reject","review_visible":true},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":749,"cache_read_input_tokens":67937,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":749},"output_tokens":57,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2dbb1532-cce8-453c-a0ba-ff5d2a8428c3","uuid":"205ff3cb-d6e8-4dcc-ae3a-d40a07b588d4"}
